# 3.1 PCB design and review

Read this reference when the schematic and main parts are established and the project needs board outline, stackup, placement, routing, copper, or DRC work.

This stage turns schematic electrical intent into the physical PCB. Stable design enters section 3.x of `CURRENT_HANDOFF.md`; complete geometry remains in the PCB source.

## Confirm inputs

At the start of PCB work, read:

- the current schematic and Contract;
- parts and exact packages;
- board outline, mounting holes, and interface positions;
- enclosure, height, and mechanical conditions;
- functional architecture and power tree;
- current, voltage, sensitivity, and topology of important nets;
- antenna, acoustic, optical, thermal, and assembly keepouts;
- test points and debug access.

An input may be confirmed, provisional, or not applicable.

Return to the previous stage only when a gap prevents current PCB work or is likely to overturn a main part, package, interface, or board outline. Converge the remaining conditions during PCB work.

## Establish the physical foundation

Determine first:

- board outline;
- mounting holes;
- edge interfaces;
- mechanical anchors;
- routing and height restrictions;
- copper layer count;
- the role of each layer;
- main reference planes;
- current manufacturing capability.

The board outline, interfaces, and mounting holes first serve the product and enclosure.

## Select the stackup

Choose the copper layer count from the actual design, considering:

- routing density;
- references for critical nets;
- power and ground planes;
- current and thermal needs;
- high-speed, RF, analog, or low-noise behavior;
- thickness, impedance, and fabrication capability;
- cost and prototype quantity.

Record each layer's role and the reference used by important signals. Physical dielectric, copper weight, and impedance values come from current fabricator capabilities or an explicit design assumption.

## Place by function

Place mechanically constrained objects first:

- connectors;
- switches and buttons;
- sensor openings;
- antennas;
- microphone and speaker interfaces;
- mounting holes;
- debug interfaces;
- user-visible parts.

Then place by functional relationship:

```text
Connector
    -> input protection
    -> power or interface circuit
    -> load or processor
```

Check that:

- protection sits near the entry;
- decoupling sits near the powered pin;
- switching power loops are compact;
- feedback and sense paths stay away from switching nodes;
- crystals, references, and sensitive analog paths remain near their parts;
- high-current, hot, and noisy circuits stay away from sensitive blocks;
- test, insertion, rework, and assembly remain accessible;
- orientation follows pin 1, interface, thermal, and routing relationships.

Placement establishes clear current and signal paths. It is not decorative alignment.

Review one critical block and the whole-board partition before completing the full placement.

## Adjust pins locally

After rough placement, a configurable part whose current assignment creates obvious crossings, detours, congestion, or vias may use the `unused_configurable_only` policy from 2.1.

After an actual change, update the Contract, schematic, manuscript, and affected firmware interface before formal routing continues.

## Build net rules

Use the schematic's electrical conditions together with the actual stackup and copper weight to determine:

- continuous and peak current;
- allowed voltage drop;
- working voltage;
- allowed temperature rise;
- route length;
- outer or inner layer;
- via count and dimensions;
- return path;
- differential, impedance, or length relationships;
- spacing from noise sources and sensitive nets.

Fabrication minimum width and spacing are process limits, not the design value for every net.

Pad escape may be narrower than the trunk, but its neck length should remain bounded. For high-current nets, inspect the narrowest point across traces, copper, vias, and connectors.

Create net classes only for requirements that actually differ.

## Route by risk

Prioritize:

1. main power and return paths;
2. switching-power loops;
3. high-speed, clock, and differential interfaces;
4. feedback, sense, and sensitive analog paths;
5. protection and fault shutdown;
6. ordinary control and low-speed digital nets.

For each critical path, check:

- implemented topology;
- branches and stubs;
- layer transitions;
- reference plane;
- return-path continuity;
- coupling to noise sources;
- vias and neck-downs;
- test-point branches.

Automatic or batch routing may handle well-bounded ordinary nets. Review the result by net purpose afterward.

## Ground, planes, and copper

Grounding follows actual current and signal returns.

Prefer a continuous reference in ordinary cases. When partitioning is needed, explain how current flows and how crossing signals return.

Check:

- decoupling loops;
- power parts and thermal pads;
- ESD discharge paths;
- reference return near signal layer changes;
- isolated ground copper and narrow channels;
- breaks caused by keepouts or slots;
- actual copper shape after pouring.

Ground vias close return, thermal, or shielding paths. Their count is not itself the goal.

For EasyEDA Pro:

- layers, outline, keepouts, and reference copper:
  [3.2-pcb-foundation.md](providers/easyeda-pro/3.2-pcb-foundation.md);
- layer-transition return and ground vias:
  [3.3-pcb-grounding.md](providers/easyeda-pro/3.3-pcb-grounding.md).

Use this order:

```text
3.2 Establish layers, outline, and functional keepouts
    -> 3.1 Complete placement and critical routing
    -> 3.2 Repour and confirm reference copper
    -> 3.3 Close return paths and grounding
    -> 3.1 Complete whole-board review
```

## Thermal and mechanical

Check:

- copper area and cooling for power parts;
- thermal vias and exposed pads;
- effects of hot parts on sensors, batteries, and enclosure;
- airflow and sealed enclosures;
- component height and assembly orientation;
- connector insertion space;
- screws, standoffs, and enclosure load;
- rework and probe access;
- acoustic openings, optical paths, airflow, and antenna regions.

Empty area may serve antenna, acoustic, thermal, assembly, or routing needs.

## Update the project manuscript

Write stable PCB conclusions under section 3.x:

- outline and mechanical conditions;
- functional partitioning and placement relationships;
- net classes, trace widths, and via strategy;
- critical topology and return paths;
- keepouts, thermal, and assembly requirements;
- current placement, routing, copper, and DRC state;
- current PCB source.

Do not copy full geometry or DRC output into the manuscript.

## Finish this stage

Before manufacturing preparation, confirm:

- the PCB source is saved;
- main parts, outline, holes, and interface positions are correct;
- all required nets are routed;
- critical power, signal, and return paths follow the current design;
- copper is current;
- pin adjustments are synchronized;
- current DRC issues are explained or resolved;
- mechanical, assembly, test, and thermal conditions are reviewed;
- section 3.x and the current handoff at the top are updated.

Handle Gerber, drill, BOM, CPL, and fabricator preview in [4.1-production-handoff.md](4.1-production-handoff.md).
